# ─── Open Harness — secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to the gitignored .env at the repo root before building:
#
#     cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# `oh init` does this for you; `oh secret set <KEY>` edits a single key
# without opening an editor.
#
# THIS FILE HOLDS SECRETS ONLY. Every NON-secret setting lives in the
# tracked oh.json — see docs/configuration.md for the field reference.
# The split is enforced in code: .oh/cli/src/lib/secrets.ts owns the
# allow-list below, and .oh/cli/src/lib/config-render.ts refuses to
# render any of these keys into the compose environment file.
#
# FORMAT: `KEY=value`, one per line, no `export`. A `#` at the start of a
# line comments it out; a `#` INSIDE a value is part of the value, not a
# comment. Quote a value only if you also want to `source` this file from
# a shell.
#
# PRECEDENCE: a variable already exported in the shell that runs `oh`
# beats the value here.
#
# Every key below is commented out, so the shipped file is inert. Apply
# changes with:  oh stop && oh sandbox

# ─── GitHub (primary git auth method) ────────────────────────────────
# Personal Access Token for GitHub CLI authentication inside the sandbox.
# On startup, entrypoint auto-runs `gh auth login`, `gh auth setup-git`,
# and (if `admin:public_key` scope is granted) generates an ed25519 SSH
# key and uploads the public key to GitHub — no interactive prompts.
# Recommended: set this for frictionless git operations and gh CLI automation.
# Without it, git and `gh` inside the sandbox stay unauthenticated until you
# run `gh auth login` by hand from a Herdr pane.
# Create one at: https://github.com/settings/tokens?type=beta
# Scopes needed: repo, read:org, admin:public_key (for SSH key upload)
# GH_TOKEN=

# ─── Sandbox user password (login + sudo) ────────────────────────────
# The `sandbox` user's password, used for BOTH remote login (SSH/`su sandbox`)
# AND sudo. Sudo is NOT passwordless (/etc/sudoers.d/sandbox is
# `sandbox ALL=(ALL) ALL`): an interactive operator who knows this password can
# escalate, but a non-interactive internal agent hits the password prompt and
# fails (`sudo -n` returns "a password is required"). With the Docker socket
# mounted (access.dockerSocket in oh.json) the sandbox user is also in the
# `docker` group, which remains an independent root-escalation path — this
# gates `sudo`, not all escalation.
# SECURITY: the compose default is weak and known; override it on any
# network-reachable deployment, from here or your platform's secret store.
# SANDBOX_PASSWORD=test1234

# ─── xAI / Grok Build ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional API key fallback for the Grok Build CLI. The preferred browser or
# device auth flow writes persistent state under ~/.grok (Docker volume), but
# this secret can be passed through for non-interactive use. Cached OAuth
# state in ~/.grok/auth.json takes precedence over this key.
# XAI_API_KEY=

# ─── Slack bot (pi-messenger-bridge) ─────────────────────────────────
# The pi-messenger-bridge package bridges Slack messages into a Pi agent
# over Socket Mode. Read from process.env at session_start; no compose
# overlay needed.
#
# Load into the shell that launches `pi` inside the sandbox:
#     set -a; source /home/sandbox/harness/.env; set +a
# `set -a` is required because this file uses Compose's KEY=value
# format (no `export`); without it, sourced vars stay in the shell
# and aren't inherited by child processes like `pi`.
#
# Runtime config (autoConnect + challenge-auth trustedUsers) lives in
# the tracked ~/.pi/msg-bridge.json (hand-edited; the entrypoint copies it
# into ~/.pi/ on boot). There is no static allowlist — access is gated by
# runtime challenge-auth. Without both tokens the bridge cannot connect.
# See docs/integrations/slack.md.
#
# Slack app-level token for Socket Mode connection (starts with xapp-).
# PI_SLACK_APP_TOKEN=xapp-...
#
# Slack bot OAuth token for posting messages (starts with xoxb-).
# PI_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...

# ─── Optional Langfuse observability for Pi only ─────────────────────
# pi-langfuse reads these only from the environment of the Pi process; they
# are not automatically injected wholesale into Pi. Claude Code uses its
# Langfuse marketplace plugin's `/plugin configure` flow instead; do not add
# Claude plugin credentials here.
# If using these Pi values, launch Pi from a shell with:
#     set -a; source /home/sandbox/harness/.env; set +a; pi
# The non-secret Langfuse settings (LANGFUSE_BASE_URL, LANGFUSE_PRIVACY_PRESET)
# are shell exports, not harness config — see docs/integrations/langfuse.md
# for Pi precedence and privacy guidance.
# LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk-lf-...
# LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=sk-lf-...

# ─── OpenHarness Cloud provisioning ──────────────────────────────────
# Provisioner key for `oh cloud`. `oh cloud config` normally stores it at
# ~/.config/openharness/cloud.json with mode 0600 and never prints it; set
# it here only for non-interactive automation that cannot run that prompt.
# Without a key, every `oh cloud` call that reaches the API fails.
# OH_CLOUD_PROVISION_KEY=
